commit 547cf1af7c8ff876db97e4dfac85d5afa0b366cd
Author:     Ayush Bhat <dev@example.com>
AuthorDate: Thu Aug 27 20:20:02 2026 +0200
Commit:     Ayush Bhat <dev@example.com>
CommitDate: Thu Aug 27 20:20:02 2026 +0200

    harden(sandbox): real userns probe, kernel-boundary flags, user-toolchain binds (#21)

    Hardens the Linux bwrap sandbox so it always works AND reports honestly.
    Mirrors the mcp-cpp fix into agentty tools (bash/hooks) + bumps mcp-cpp.

    1. REAL PROBE (the correctness fix). The old check (bwrap --version) only
       proved the binary exists; it passed on hosts where bwrap is installed but
       unprivileged user namespaces are BLOCKED (Ubuntu 24.04 AppArmor userns
       restriction, kernel.unprivileged_userns_clone=0). Every real command then
       died with "setting up uid map: Permission denied" while agentty reported
       "sandbox: active" (issue #21). Now bwrap_can_sandbox() runs a genuine
       minimal sandbox against /bin/true and requires it to start + exit 0, so a
       blocked-userns host is detected: Auto degrades to unsandboxed (commands
       work) and On refuses with a clear message.

    2. STRONGER FLAGS. --unshare-user (explicit, matches the probe) +
       --unshare-ipc/uts/cgroup-try alongside the existing pid/new-session/
       die-with-parent. No ambient caps, no_new_privs (bwrap default in a userns).

    3. USER TOOLCHAINS (the other half of #21). Bind ~/.local/bin, ~/.local/opt,
       ~/.cargo/bin, ~/.rustup, ~/go/bin, ~/.nvm, pyenv/rbenv/asdf, bun/deno, etc.
       READ-ONLY so approved commands find tools installed outside /usr (the
       webinstall.dev go/gofmt case). Narrow tool-only roots; secret/app-data
       dirs (~/.ssh, ~/.aws, ~/.config, ~/.local/share, ~/.npm) are NEVER bound.

    describe_state distinguishes "bwrap missing" from "userns blocked" and points
    the user at the fix. Docs updated with the toolchain list, hardening flags,
    and an honest "if bwrap cant build a namespace" section. New test
    (bwrap_argv_for_test) pins every hardening flag + toolchain bind + the
    secret-exclusion, on any host. 331/331 green; verified live that the hardened
    argv runs and a ~/.local/bin tool resolves inside the sandbox.
